    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `check_server(server, cases)` in `mcpcheck/inproc.py`, and all 5 tests pass with `pytest -q`, including 3 new ones. One of them runs the same cases against the same server both ways, over stdio with `check_command` and in-process with `check_server`, and the results are identical.\n\n**How it works.** The pinned SDK (mcp 2.2.0) has a `Client` that can connect to a server object directly in memory, with no subprocess and no sockets. I use it like this:\n\n```python\nasync with Client(server, mode=\"legacy\", cache=None) as client:\n    session = client.session\n    for case in cases:\n        result = await session.call_tool(case.tool, case.arguments)\n        ...\n```\n\nThe comparison is the same `compare()` from `mcpcheck/cases.py`, and mismatches come back in case order, exactly like `check_command`.\n\n**Two settings matter:**\n- **`mode=\"legacy\"`**: this makes the in-process connection run the same message loop and `initialize()` handshake that `check_command` uses over stdio. The default mode skips both for in-process servers, so tool results and errors could differ from what stdio returns.\n- **`cache=None`**: `Client` caches some responses by default, and this turns that off so every case really reaches the server. The loop also calls `client.session.call_tool` directly, the same call `check_command` makes, so the cache couldn't interfere anyway.\n\nNo dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests** are in `tests/test_inproc.py` and use a small server built with the decorator API:\n- Mismatches come back in order with the actual text the tool returned.\n- A run where every case passes returns an empty list.\n- A tool that raises an exception is reported as a mismatch holding the server's error text, the same as over stdio.\n\nFor server authors, usage in their own suite is `anyio.run(check_server, server, load_cases(\"cases.toml\"))`, or `await check_server(...)` inside an async test."
  },
